---

## Test Smell Audit — `UserRepositoryIntegrationTests.cs`

**Framework:** MSTest 4.1.0 · **.NET 10** · **Integration test suite** (`[TestCategory("Integration")]`)

---

### Summary Dashboard

| Severity | Smell Count | Affected Tests |
|----------|-------------|----------------|
| High     | 2           | 2              |
| Medium   | 2           | 2              |
| Low      | 0           | 0              |
| **Total**| **4**       | **4** (of 8)   |

---

### Findings by Severity

#### 🔴 HIGH 1 — Conditional Test Logic

**Test:** `GetUser_ReturnsCorrectType` (line 92)

```csharp
if (loaded is PremiumUser premium)
{
    Assert.IsTrue(premium.DiscountRate > 0);
}
else
{
    Assert.AreEqual("Eve", loaded!.Name);
}
```

**Problem:** The `if/else` means one assertion branch *never* executes. If `loaded` is never a `PremiumUser`, the discount-rate check is dead code — you'd never know it's broken. This smell is not mitigated by integration-test calibration; conditional logic hides gaps regardless of test scope.

**Fix — split into two tests with explicit setup:**
```csharp
[TestMethod]
public void GetUser_StandardUser_ReturnsCorrectName()
{
    _repository.Insert(new User("eve@example.com", "Eve"));
    var loaded = _repository.GetByEmail("eve@example.com");
    Assert.IsNotNull(loaded);
    Assert.AreEqual("Eve", loaded.Name);
}

[TestMethod]
public void GetUser_PremiumUser_HasPositiveDiscount()
{
    _repository.Insert(new PremiumUser("eve@example.com", "Eve", discountRate: 0.1m));
    var loaded = (PremiumUser)_repository.GetByEmail("eve@example.com")!;
    Assert.IsTrue(loaded.DiscountRate > 0);
}
```

**Risk if unfixed:** Silent coverage gap — one branch is never tested, giving false confidence.

---

#### 🔴 HIGH 2 — Assertion-Free Test (Unknown Test)

**Test:** `BulkInsert_RunsWithoutErrors` (line 112)

```csharp
public void BulkInsert_RunsWithoutErrors()
{
    for (int i = 0; i < 100; i++)
    {
        _repository.Insert(new User($"bulk{i}@example.com", $"Bulk User {i}"));
    }
    // ← no assertion
}
```

**Problem:** This test passes as long as no exception is thrown. It doesn't verify the 100 users were actually persisted. The data could silently vanish and this test would still be green.

**Fix — assert the outcome:**
```csharp
[TestMethod]
public void BulkInsert_PersistsAllRecords()
{
    for (int i = 0; i < 100; i++)
    {
        _repository.Insert(new User($"bulk{i}@example.com", $"Bulk User {i}"));
    }

    var users = _repository.ListAll();
    Assert.AreEqual(100, users.Count);
}
```

**Risk if unfixed:** False confidence — test counts toward coverage but verifies nothing.

---

#### 🟡 MEDIUM 1 — Sleepy Test

**Test:** `NotifyOnInsert_SendsEventAfterDelay` (line 81)

```csharp
public async Task NotifyOnInsert_SendsEventAfterDelay()
{
    var user = new User("dave@example.com", "Dave");
    _repository.Insert(user);

    Thread.Sleep(3000);   // ← blocks for 3 seconds

    Assert.IsTrue(_repository.WasNotificationSent(user.Id));
}
```

**Problem:** A hard 3-second sleep makes this test slow and non-deterministic. If the notification takes 3.1 s on a loaded CI machine, you get a flaky failure. Also, `Thread.Sleep` in an `async` method blocks the thread instead of yielding it.

**Calibration note:** Downgraded from High → Medium because this is an integration test where some async coordination is expected. However, a polling/retry approach is still preferred.

**Fix — poll with timeout:**
```csharp
[TestMethod]
public async Task NotifyOnInsert_SendsEventAfterDelay()
{
    var user = new User("dave@example.com", "Dave");
    _repository.Insert(user);

    var timeout = TimeSpan.FromSeconds(5);
    var interval = TimeSpan.FromMilliseconds(100);
    var sw = Stopwatch.StartNew();

    while (sw.Elapsed < timeout)
    {
        if (_repository.WasNotificationSent(user.Id))
        {
            Assert.IsTrue(true);
            return;
        }
        await Task.Delay(interval);
    }

    Assert.Fail("Notification was not sent within the timeout.");
}
```

**Risk if unfixed:** Flaky CI failures and a 3-second drag on every suite run.

---

#### 🟡 MEDIUM 2 — Assertion Roulette

**Test:** `InsertUser_PersistsToDatabase` (line 28)

```csharp
Assert.IsNotNull(loaded);
Assert.AreEqual("Alice", loaded.Name);
Assert.AreEqual("alice@example.com", loaded.Email);
Assert.IsTrue(loaded.Id > 0);
```

**Problem:** Four assertions with no message parameters. When one fails, MSTest reports the line number but not *why* that value was expected. On a CI log you see `Assert.AreEqual failed. Expected:<Alice>. Actual:<null>` with no business context.

**Fix — add message parameters:**
```csharp
Assert.IsNotNull(loaded, "User should be retrievable after insert");
Assert.AreEqual("Alice", loaded.Name, "Name should be persisted");
Assert.AreEqual("alice@example.com", loaded.Email, "Email should be persisted");
Assert.IsTrue(loaded.Id > 0, "Auto-generated Id should be positive");
```

**Risk if unfixed:** Slower triage when failures occur in CI — developers must read source to understand what failed.

---

### Extended Catalog — Clean

The remaining 15 smells from the testsmells.org taxonomy were checked and **not found**:

| Smell | Status |
|-------|--------|
| Mystery Guest | ✅ Clean — in-memory SQLite is hermetic; appropriate for integration tests |
| Eager Test | ✅ Clean — no test calls 4+ distinct production methods |
| Magic Number Test | ✅ Clean — `3` after adding 3 items and `10` after inserting 10 are contextually obvious |
| Sensitive Equality | ✅ Clean — no `.ToString()` comparisons |
| Exception Handling | ✅ Clean — no `try/catch` in tests |
| General Fixture | ✅ Clean — `_connection` and `_repository` are used by every test |
| Ignored/Disabled Test | ✅ Clean — no skipped tests |
| Duplicate Assert | ✅ Clean |
| Lazy Test | ✅ Clean — shared method calls are setup, not the SUT action |
| Constructor Initialization | ✅ Clean — uses `[TestInitialize]` correctly |
| Default Test | ✅ Clean — class has a descriptive name |
| Redundant Print | ✅ Clean — no `Console.WriteLine` |
| Redundant Assertion | ✅ Clean — no tautological assertions |
| Resource Optimism | ✅ Clean — no assumed external resources |
| Empty Test | ✅ Clean |

### Smell-Free Tests Worth Highlighting

`DeleteUser_RemovesFromDatabase`, `ListUsers_ReturnsAllInserted`, and `InsertUser_ConcurrentInserts_MaintainsIntegrity` are well-structured: clear arrange/act/assert, contextually obvious values, and appropriate assertions. These are good templates for future tests.

---

### Prioritized Remediation Plan

| Priority | Smell | Test | Effort | Why First |
|----------|-------|------|--------|-----------|
| 1 | Assertion-Free Test | `BulkInsert_RunsWithoutErrors` | ~2 min | Prevents false-pass; one line to add |
| 2 | Conditional Test Logic | `GetUser_ReturnsCorrectType` | ~10 min | Eliminates dead-code branch; split into two tests |
| 3 | Sleepy Test | `NotifyOnInsert_SendsEventAfterDelay` | ~10 min | Removes flakiness + 3s drag |
| 4 | Assertion Roulette | `InsertUser_PersistsToDatabase` | ~2 min | Improves triage speed; add message strings |

**Bottom line:** 4 of 8 tests have smells. The two High findings (assertion-free test and conditional logic) are the most urgent — they create genuine blind spots. The suite's foundation is solid: hermetic fixtures, good naming, and clean separation of concerns.
